{"type":"thinking","text":"$0776 = 1 (paused). $0770 = 1 (game mode). $0778 = 3, $0779 = 0, $077A=0, $077B=0, $077C=1, $077D=0. Hmm.\n\nTimer $07F8-$07FA = [3,8,4] → TIME 384? But screenshot shows TIME 384! Wait, screenshot shows TIME 384. So the game restarted the level (Mario died from time-up at 005, respawned with TIME 384?) Actually when Mario dies from time up, he loses a life and restarts 1-1 with TIME 400? Displayed 384 maybe mid-counting before pause. Anyway.\n\nThe pause via $0776=1: the game is paused now. Timer 384. Let me verify it stays frozen: I'll read again later. \n\nNow, is the RNG frozen too? If the RNG updates in the game engine (which is skipped when paused), it's frozen. Good. If it updates in the NMI, it keeps going.\n\nNow, my plan for the RNG:\nOption A: Find a routine in PRG that generates random numbers. Let me search for the \"random\" usage in the game engine.\n\nOption B: Differential search triggered by a random event.\n\nLet me think about what's easily reproducible that uses RNG in 1-1. Hmm. In 1-1, the \"? block\" item is fixed. The Goomba behavior is deterministic. The \"hidden 1-up block\"? Its location is fixed.\n\nHmm, what about the \"floating coin\" from a ? block? Deterministic.\n\nActually, maybe SMB's RNG is used for the \"spinning\" of the \"flower\" or the \"star\"? Hmm.\n\nWait — actually the most famous SMB randomness: the \"random\" behavior of the \"Piranha Plant\" in pipes, and the \"Cheep Cheep\" in water levels, and the \"Hammer Bro\" hammers in 1-3? but we're in 1-1. Also the \"Goomba\" doesn't.\n\nHmm, but the RNG STATE likely advances every frame regardless (many games do). Let me test: with the game paused ($0776=1), does the \"RNG\" (candidate cluster) change? If yes, it's NMI-driven. Let me read $07A7-$07AD now vs earlier. Earlier (before pause) $07A7=105,$07A8=223,$07A9=12,$07AA=178,$07AB=171,$07AC=206,$07AD=153. Let me read now.\n\nActually, the level restarted (death), so those values may have reset.\n\nLet me read $07A0-$07AF now. And also read $0009 twice to see if NMI keeps running.\n\nLet me do read $07A0 length 16 and read $0009. Then again later.\n\nHmm, this is getting long. Let me step back and think about the most likely answer.\n\nGiven \"sheet 3 (the RAM a hacker reaches for)\" and SMB, the two requested addresses are likely the canonical ones from the SMB RAM map. The controller: $06FC (SavedJoypad1Bits) - done. The RNG: In the SMB RAM map, there's a variable often called \"Random number generator\" ... Let me recall the actual disassembly variable list from the SMB source (smbdis.asm by doppelganger):\n\n```\n;-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n; $00-$FF  PAGE ZERO\n...\n;$0F        - Used for area type and other functions\n```\n\nHmm, the SMB zero page usage:\n- $00-$01: pointer\n- $02-$03\n- $04-$05\n- $06-$07\n- $0E: Player_State\n- $0F: ? \n- $10-$14: axis velocity\n- ...\n\nActually the SMB RAM map I recall:\n```\n$0000-0001  ; used for data pointer\n$0002-0003\n$0004-0005\n$0006-0007\n$0008       ; area type?\n$0009       ; frame counter? no...\n```\n\nHmm.\n\nActually, I now recall the SMB disassembly has:\n```\nFrameCounter = $09\n...\nRandomNum = $07A3   ; no, search says nothing\n```\n\nHmm, search for `A3 07` found nothing, but maybe the code accesses it via indexed addressing or the address is different.\n\nLet me search for the known constant. What about the possibility that the SMB RNG is at $07A7? Wait, $07A7 is documented as a resume marker ($07FF and $07A7 = $A5). Hmm.\n\nHold on, the mapping says \"$8036 STA $07FF=$A5 and $8039 $07A7=$A5 (write the resume markers)\". So $07A7 = $A5 is set at boot. But in the diff, $07A7 changed from 206 to 250 (0xCE→0xFA) and later read 105. So $07A7 is NOT a constant marker during gameplay; it's something else (maybe the boot marker is only checked/set once).\n\nHmm, actually the boot code sets $07A7=$A5, but during the level it's changed. So my mapping's note about $07A7 being a marker might be wrong (or it's reused).\n\nOK let me just do the empirical thing with the watch on the candidate cluster plus others, but I need a controlled interval. The game is now paused ($0776=1). If I snapshot now (e0), then unpause by writing $0776=0 and run play_input for exactly N frames, then... the snapshot after would be uncontrolled.\n\nAlternative: use play_input watch on the candidates while the game runs. Let me watch $07A7-$07AD and $0009 over a 60-frame run and see the sequences. If one of them increments in a pseudo-random pattern, that's the RNG.\n\nBut wait: with $0776=1, the engine is paused, so nothing advances except NMI stuff ($0009). I need to unpause. Let me write $0776=0 then immediately watch.\n\nActually play_input will run frames; if $0776=0 the engine runs. Let me:\n1. write $0776=0 (unpause)\n2. play_input watch [9,1959,1960,1961,1962,1963,1964,1965,1748,773,1919,1927,2041,2042] for 60 frames.\n\nThe watch samples each frame (or every 2). Let me look at the sequences.\n\nLet me convert:\n9, 1959,1960,1961,1962,1963,1964,1965, 1748, 773, 1919, 1927, 2041, 2042. That's 14.\n\nLet me run.\n\nActually, I realize I should also think: the RNG in SMB might be updated only when a \"random\" is requested. In that case, watching during idle won't show it. But the initial diff DID show $07A7-$07AD changing during idle. So something in that cluster changes during idle. Let me watch and see.\n\nLet me unpause and watch."}
