{"type":"thinking","text":"Now this is very revealing. Look at addresses $07A7-$07AD: they are a *shift register*! \n\n$07A7 values: 102,179,89,44,22,139,69,...\n$07A8: 196,98,177,216,...\n$07A9: 9,4,2,129,64,32,16,136,196,98,177,...\n\nLook at $07A9: 9,4,2,129,64,32,16,136,196,98,177,216,108,54,155,205,102,179,89,44,22,139,69,162,209,232,244,122,189,222,111,183,91,173,86,171,85,42,21,138,69,162,209,232,244,122,61,30,15,135,67,161,80,40,148,74,37,146,73,164\n\nAnd $07A8: 196,98,177,216,108,54,155,205,102,179,89,44,22,139,69,162,209,232,244,122,189,222,111,183,91,173,86,171,85,42,21,138,69,162,209,232,244,122,61,30,15,135,67,161,80,40,148,74,37,146,73,164,82,169,84,42,21,10,5,2\n$07A7: 102,179,89,44,22,139,69,162,209,232,244,122,189,222,111,183,91,173,86,171,85,42,21,138,69,162,209,232,244,122,61,30,15,135,67,161,80,40,148,74,37,146,73,164,82,169,84,42,21,10,5,2,129,192,96,48,152,76,166,211\n\nNotice: $07A7 at frame k equals $07A8 at frame k-1? $07A7 frame2=179, $07A8 frame1=196? No.\n\nActually look: $07A8 sequence from frame 1: 196,98,177,216,108,54,155,205,102,179,89,44,22,139,...\n$07A9 from frame 1: 9,4,2,129,64,32,16,136,196,98,177,216,108,...\n$07A7 from frame 1: 102,179,89,44,22,139,69,162,209,232,244,122,189,222,...\n\nHmm, look at $07A7: 102,179,89,44,22,139,69,162,209,232,244,122,189,222,111,183,91,173,86,171,85,42,21,138,69,162,209,232,244,122,61,30,15,135,67,161,80,40,148,74,37,146,73,164,82,169,84,42,21,10,5,2,129,192,96,48,152,76,166,211\n\nAnd $07A8 frame 2 onward: 98,177,216,108,54,155,205,102,179,89,44,22,139,69,162,209,232,244,122,189,222,111,183,91,173,86,171,85,42,21,138,69,162,209,232,244,122,61,30,15,135,67,161,80,40,148,74,37,146,73,164,82,169,84,42,21,10,5,2...\n\nSo $07A7 at frame k+1 = $07A8 at frame k? $07A8 frame1=196, $07A7 frame2=179. No.\n\nHmm, let's see the transformation. Take 179: it becomes 89 (179>>1 = 89), then 44 (89>>1), then 22, then 139 (22>>1=11, hmm no: 22>>1 = 11, but next is 139). Hmm: 22 → 139? 22 = 0b00010110. 139 = 0b10001011. That's (22>>1)|0x80 = 11|128 = 139. Yes! So when the shifted-out bit (bit0) is 0, the LSB becomes 0; when bit0 is 1... wait: 179 = 0b10110011, >>1 = 0b01011001 = 89. Then 89>>1 = 44 (0b00101100); 44>>1=22; 22>>1=11 → but we got 139 = 11|128. So the rule: if bit0 of previous was 0, we set bit7. Hmm, 22 bit0=0 → 139 = (22>>1)|0x80. 44 bit0=0 → should set bit7: 44>>1=22, but we got 22 (no bit7). Inconsistent.\n\nLet me look at the sequence more carefully. It's clearly an LFSR. The values form a maximal-length sequence? Let's check for the characteristic polynomial.\n\nConsider the sequence in $07A9: 9,4,2,129,64,32,16,136,196,98,177,216,108,54,155,205,102,179,89,44,22,139,69,162,209,232,244,122,189,222,111,183,91,173,86,171,85,42,21,138,69,162,...\n\nLet's test the recurrence: x(n+1) = (x(n) >> 1) ^ (0x?? if x(n)&1). \n9 = 0b00001001, LSB=1. 9>>1=4, then XOR something gives 4, so XOR value = 0. Hmm that gives 4. Next: 4 LSB=0 → 4>>1=2 → 2. Next 2 LSB=0 → 1, but we got 129. Hmm.\n\nMaybe it's left-shift: x(n+1) = (x<<1) ^ (0x?? if bit7). 9<<1=18, but next is 4. No.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe it's a right-shift where the new MSB is XOR of some taps. 9=1001: if taps are bits 0 and 4? New bit = bit0 XOR ... hmm.\n\nActually 9 → 4 (9>>1=4). Then 4 → 2 (4>>1). Then 2 → 129: 2>>1=1, plus 128. So new MSB set when previous value was 2 (bit0=0). Hmm, so the feedback is not just from bit0.\n\nLet me try: x(n+1) = (x(n) >> 1) | (feedback << 7), where feedback = XOR of certain bits of x(n). For 9=00001001 → 4=00000100: feedback=0. For 4=00000100 → 2=00000010: feedback=0. For 2=00000010 → 129=10000001: feedback=1, and 2>>1=1. For 129=10000001 → 64=01000000: feedback=0. 129>>1=64. For 64 → 32: feedback 0. 32→16: 0. 16=00010000 → 136=10001000: feedback=1, 16>>1=8 → 136. For 136=10001000 → 196=11000100: feedback=1, 136>>1=68=01000100, |128=196. yes. 196=11000100 → 98=01100010: feedback=0? 196>>1=98. yes feedback=0. But wait 196 has bit7=1. Hmm, so feedback isn't simply bit7.