{"type":"thinking","text":"Both mappings promoted. Now give the final report.\n\nLet me summarize:\n1. Controller 1 buttons: $06FC (7-bit... actually 8-bit flags). Encoding: bit7=A $80, bit6=B $40, bit5=Select $20, bit4=Start $10, bit3=Up $08, bit2=Down $04, bit1=Left $02, bit0=Right $01. Writer: ReadJoypads at $8E5C, STA $06FC,X at $8E7B. Proof: per-frame watch following the button schedule exactly: 0x00, 0x01, 0x02, 0x81, 0x40, 0x08, 0x04 for none, Right, Left, Right+A, B, Up, Down; plus Select 0x20 and Start 0x10 on their first frames ($074A edge-mask clears held Start/Select after one frame).\n\n2. RNG state: $07A7-$07AD (base $07A7), 7-byte shift register, updated every frame by the NMI at $811B-$8136. Feedback = bit1($07A7) XOR bit1($07A8); ROR $07A7,X for X=0..6. Proof: trace (only writer PC $8134/prgRom 0x134), per-frame watch, and the recurrence verified exactly against the samples. Consumers read $07A8,X (X=2..4 → $07AA-$07AC) at CPU $B9C9.\n\nReport shape per skill: say the address, the encoding, how many survivors each round left, and what the proof showed. Quote raw diff for the last round.\n\nThe diff round: idle snapshots e0/e1 \"changed\" → 14 survivors. I should quote that. The 14 addresses: $0009, $0305, $06D4, $077F, $0787, $07A7-$07AD, $07F9, $07FA.\n\nThen the watch isolated the LFSR.\n\nLet me also note the game state at the end: running.\n\nI'll write a concise final report.\n\nAlso, per the skill, mention that lookup_symbol wasn't used (no reference). And promote_mappings called once (2 discoveries promoted).\n\nLet me write it."}
{"type":"text","text":"Both discoveries are recorded and promoted (2 promoted; the mapping file now has 19 addresses). Here's what I found and how I proved it.\n\n## 1. Controller 1 buttons — `$06FC`\n\n**Encoding (flags):** bit7 = A `$80`, bit6 = B `$40`, bit5 = Select `$20`, bit4 = Start `$10`, bit3 = Up `$08`, bit2 = Down `$04`, bit1 = Left `$02`, bit0 = Right `$01`; `0` = nothing. `$06FD` is the same for player 2. This is the byte the game keeps for the current frame (\"SavedJoypad1Bits\"); it is rewritten every frame, so a RAM write cannot stick — a watch is the proof.\n\n**Proof:** disassembly showed the NMI's joypad routine `ReadJoypads` at `$8E5C` strobes `$4016` and ends with `STA $06FC,X` (`$8E7B`). A per-frame `play_input` watch of `$06FC` tracked the script exactly:\n\n| buttons held | `$06FC` |\n|---|---|\n| none | `00` |\n| Right | `01` |\n| Left | `02` |\n| Right + A | `81` |\n| B | `40` |\n| Up | `08` |\n| Down | `04` |\n\nA second run with 1-frame sampling caught the edge-pulses: `$06FC` = `20` on Select's first frame and `10` on Start's, with the game's `$074A` mask clearing a *held* Start/Select after one frame (that's why a long Select hold reads `00`).\n\n## 2. Pseudo-random number generator state — `$07A7` (7 bytes, `$07A7`–`$07AD`)\n\n**Encoding:** a 7-byte (56-bit) shift register. Every frame the NMI computes feedback = bit1(`$07A7`) XOR bit1(`$07A8`) at `$811F`–`$8130`, then runs `ROR $07A7,X` for X=0..6 (`$8131`–`$8136`, `BNE $8131`), so the whole register rotates right one bit with the feedback entering bit7 of `$07A7`, the carry chaining byte-to-byte, and bit0 of `$07AD` discarded.\n\n**Proof:**\n- `trace_writes` (write) on `$07A7`–`$07AD` for 60 frames returned a **single** writer, PC `$8134` (the `INX` right after `ROR $07A7,X`), prgRom 0x134 — i.e. the NMI updates all 7 bytes every frame (and this block is *not* gated by the pause flag, which is checked later at `$8144`).\n- The differential round left **14 survivors** (`changed`, idle s0→s1): `$0009, $0305, $06D4, $077F, $0787, $07A7,$07A8,$07A9,$07AA,$07AB,$07AC,$07AD, $07F9, $07FA`. A per-frame watch showed the `$07A7`–`$07AD` group was the only one running an LFSR; the rest were plain counters (`$0009` +1/frame, `$077F`/`$0787` countdowns).\n- I verified the exact recurrence against the watch samples: state1 `[102,196,9,129,146,145,180]` → state2 `[179,98,4,192,201,72,218]` → state3 `[89,177,2,96,100,164,109]`, matching every byte frame-for-frame (e.g. `$07A7` 102→179→89→44→22, `$07AC` 145→72→164→82).\n- `trace_writes` (read) found the consumer at CPU `$B9C9`, `LDA $07A8,X` with X=2..4 → it consumes the random value from `$07AA`–`$07AC` each frame and masks it (`$B9BA`) to pick a branch.\n\nFor a hacker: seed/replace the RNG by writing `$07A7`–`$07AD`; the value the game actually rolls on is read from `$07AA`–`$07AC`.\n\nBoth were found with no reference (no `lookup_symbol`), by differential search plus per-frame watches and trace, and recorded with `source: \"differential search\"`. The game is running normally in World 1-1."}
